# XSH pthread_detach()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Every call names a joinable thread (anything else is undefined), and a
# detached thread's ID is not used again (XSH 2.9.2).

assertion  XSH.pthread_detach.001
anchor     functions/pthread_detach.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_detach.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       pthread_detach() of a live joinable thread returns 0 and does not
           end it: the thread, waiting at a cancellation point when
           detached, then runs on to the end of its start routine.
note       A cleanup handler in the thread shows whether it was canceled
           instead.
test       t/001-live.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_detach.002
anchor     functions/pthread_detach.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       pthread_detach() of a joinable thread that has (in all
           likelihood) terminated, a zombie thread, returns 0.
note       The thread signals just before returning and the caller waits
           200 ms more; a thread not quite ended is a live joinable thread,
           for which the assertion holds too.
test       t/002-zombie.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_detach.003
anchor     functions/pthread_detach.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A thread can detach itself: pthread_detach(pthread_self())
           returns 0 and the thread goes on running.
test       t/003-self.c
